---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: author.name,
    section: "Brand Reviews",
    tags: [
      "kachava protein powder",
      "ka'chava",
      "kachava review",
      "kachava meal shake"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
  stickyCta={{
    text: "See Our Top Plant Protein Picks",
    link: "/best-plant-based-protein-powder/",
  }}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <ArticleHeader kicker="Brand Review" badge="Label-Checked" author={author} dates={dates} mobileAd={false}>
      <Fragment slot="title">Ka'Chava Review: Meal Shake or Protein Powder?</Fragment>
      Ka'Chava sells a 240-calorie superfood meal shake with 25 g of plant protein. We read the label, priced it per 25 g of protein and compared it with plain protein powders and rival meal shakes.
    </ArticleHeader>

    <TableOfContents>
      <a href="#verdict">The Short Verdict</a>
      <a href="#what-it-is">What Ka'Chava Actually Is</a>
      <a href="#top-picks">Flavors and Alternatives</a>
      <a href="#label">The Label, Line by Line</a>
      <a href="#cost">Cost per 25 g of Protein</a>
      <a href="#vs-protein">Ka'Chava vs Plain Protein</a>
      <a href="#vs-meal-shakes">Ka'Chava vs Huel</a>
      <a href="#testing">Testing and Safety</a>
      <a href="#who">Who Should Skip It</a>
      <a href="#where-to-buy">Where to Buy</a>
      <a href="#mistakes">Common Mistakes</a>
      <a href="#faq">FAQ</a>
    </TableOfContents>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="lede">
          Ka'Chava is a good meal shake and an expensive protein powder. If you want a vegan breakfast in a glass with fiber, greens and a multivitamin built in, it does that job well. If you just want 25 g of protein after the gym, you are paying roughly three times what a plain plant protein costs for the same protein.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="verdict" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The Short Verdict
          </h2>

          <p>
            Ka'Chava is worth buying if you will use it to replace a meal you currently skip or eat badly. One serving of two scoops gives about 240 calories, 25 g of plant protein, around 6 g of fiber and a long list of vitamins, minerals, greens, adaptogens, probiotics and digestive enzymes, per the label at the time of writing. That is a reasonable breakfast for a lot of people, and it is far better than a pastry and a latte.
          </p>

          <p>
            It is not worth buying as a protein supplement. At around $70 for a 15-serving bag bought one at a time, or about $60 on subscription, you pay roughly $4 to $4.70 for each 25 g of protein. A plain pea or pea-and-rice protein typically delivers the same 25 g for well under half of that. If protein is the only reason you are reading this, you can stop here and go straight to our <a href="/best-plant-based-protein-powder/">best plant-based protein powder</a> picks.
          </p>

          <p>
            The middle ground is where most buyers actually sit: people who want a convenient, filling vegan shake and like the idea of greens and superfoods without buying five separate tubs. For that buyer, Ka'Chava is a defensible luxury. Its weaknesses are the price, the proprietary blends that hide how much of each superfood you actually get, a few grams of added sugar from coconut nectar, and no NSF or Informed Choice certification that we could find.
          </p>

          <p>
            Our ranking of the range is simple. Chocolate is the flagship and the flavor buyers most consistently rate highly, so it is the safe first bag. Vanilla is the most flexible base for blending with fruit. The newer fruit and dessert flavors are fun but are better bought as a second bag once you know you like the texture, which buyers often describe as thicker and more earthy than a whey shake.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="what-it-is" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. What Ka'Chava Actually Is
          </h2>

          <p>
            Ka'Chava is made by Tribal Nutrition, a company founded by Simon Malone, and it is marketed as an all-in-one nutrition shake rather than a protein powder. The distinction matters. A protein powder is designed to deliver as much protein as possible for as few calories as possible. A meal shake is designed to stand in for food, so it carries carbohydrates, fat, fiber and micronutrients along with the protein.
          </p>

          <p>
            That is why the numbers look the way they do. A typical whey isolate gives about 25 g of protein in roughly 110 to 120 calories. Ka'Chava gives the same 25 g in about 240 calories, because the other half of the calories come from coconut milk, oats, chia, flax, fruit powders and a little coconut flower nectar. Judged as a protein powder that is inefficient. Judged as a meal, 240 calories is on the light side, and many people add a banana, nut butter or oat milk to make it a full breakfast.
          </p>

          <p>
            The brand describes the formula as containing more than 85 superfoods and nutrients, grouped into blends: a plant protein blend, an adaptogen and mushroom blend, a super-fruit antioxidant blend, an omega and fiber blend, a greens blend, digestive enzymes, and a probiotic and prebiotic blend, plus a vitamin and mineral premix. It is vegan and made without dairy, gluten or soy, per the brand, though it does contain coconut, so anyone with a coconut allergy should skip it.
          </p>

          <p>
            The flavor range has grown. The long-running core flavors are chocolate and vanilla, with chai, coconut acai and matcha alongside them. Strawberry arrived in 2025 as the brand's first new flavor in three years, followed by a decaffeinated coffee flavor in March 2026 and cinnamon French toast in August 2026, with salted caramel and seasonal limited editions such as chocolate mint in between. Availability of individual flavors changes, so check the current list before you subscribe.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Ka'Chava Flavors and Alternatives Worth Buying
          </h2>

          <p>
            Ka'Chava sells one formula in many flavors, so choosing a Ka'Chava product is really choosing a flavor and a bag size. Below are the three flavors we would start with, based on the label and on consistent buyer feedback, plus two clearly labelled alternatives from other brands for readers who want more protein per meal or a much lower cost per gram. Nutrition figures are per the label at the time of writing and vary slightly by flavor.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <ProductCard product={product} rank={index + 1} />
          ))}
        </div>

        <div id="label" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. The Label, Line by Line
          </h2>

          <p>
            Start with the protein. The plant protein blend lists yellow pea protein, organic brown rice protein, organic amaranth, organic quinoa and organic sacha inchi, per the label at the time of writing. Pea and rice together give a reasonably complete amino acid profile, and that pairing is the backbone of most good vegan proteins. Amaranth, quinoa and sacha inchi are nutritious, but in practice they contribute relatively little protein next to the pea and rice.
          </p>

          <p>
            The catch is that the blend is proprietary. Ka'Chava publishes the total protein, 25 g per serving, but not how much of it comes from each source, and reviewers have noted that the brand does not publish a full amino acid breakdown. That makes it impossible to calculate leucine per serving, the amino acid most closely tied to muscle protein synthesis. Pea-and-rice blends at 25 g usually land around 2 g of leucine, which is adequate, but we cannot confirm Ka'Chava's figure.
          </p>

          <p>
            The same problem applies to the headline superfoods. The adaptogen blend of maca, reishi, shiitake, maitake, cordyceps and ginger totals about 1,020 mg across all six ingredients, per one detailed label review. Studies on adaptogens typically use doses of individual ingredients that are larger than what a 1 g blend of six can contain, so treat these as flavor and marketing rather than a functional dose. The probiotic strains are named, but the CFU count is not disclosed.
          </p>

          <p>
            On sugar, expect about 8 g of total sugar per serving, of which roughly 4 to 6 g is added, varying by flavor. The added sugar comes from organic coconut flower nectar, and the formula is also sweetened with lo han, better known as monk fruit extract. There is no sucralose, aspartame or stevia, per the brand and retailer listings. Fiber runs around 6 g per serving, which is genuinely useful: most adults fall well short of their daily fiber target, and 6 g in a breakfast shake is a meaningful contribution.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE LABEL IN ONE LINE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">25 g protein, 240 calories, 6 g fiber, 4-6 g added sugar</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Per the label at the time of writing, varying by flavor. The protein is a pea-led plant blend; the superfoods sit in proprietary blends without individual doses; and sweetness comes from coconut flower nectar and monk fruit.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            The vitamin and mineral premix is broad, which is part of the appeal for people who would otherwise take a multivitamin. If you already take one, or you drink two Ka'Chava shakes a day, check the totals for vitamin A, iron and zinc against your other supplements, and if you are pregnant, take medication or have a medical condition, run the label past your doctor or a registered dietitian first. This is general information, not medical advice.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Cost per 25 g of Protein
          </h2>

          <p>
            The cleanest way to compare a meal shake with a protein powder is cost per 25 g of protein, because that is roughly what one good shake should deliver. Ka'Chava makes this easy: one serving is 25 g of protein. A 15-serving bag on the brand's own site has listed at around $70 bought once, or about $60 on subscription, which works out to roughly $4.65 and $4.00 per 25 g respectively. Prices change, so treat these as approximate.
          </p>

          <p>
            Retail sizes complicate the comparison. Target and other stores sell a smaller bag of about 15.3 oz, or roughly seven servings, and in 2026 Ka'Chava began selling a 20-serving chocolate bag at selected Costco warehouses in the Los Angeles area. Single-serve sachets are available for trying flavors. Always divide the price by the number of servings on the label rather than comparing bag prices, because the bags are not the same size.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Product</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Protein per serving</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Calories</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Approx. cost per 25 g protein</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Ka'Chava (15-serving bag)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">25 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 240</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $4.00-$4.70</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Huel Black Edition</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">40 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 400</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Varies; check current price</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Orgain Organic Plant Protein</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">21 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 150</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Typically well under half of Ka'Chava</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Typical pea-rice protein</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">20-25 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 110-130</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Typically $1.00-$2.00</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            That gap is the whole argument of this review. If you put a scoop of plain pea protein, a tablespoon of chia, a handful of spinach and a banana in a blender, you will get similar protein and more fiber for less than half the cost, though you lose the convenience and the multivitamin. Whether the convenience is worth an extra $2 to $3 per shake every day is a personal call, but it adds up to roughly $700 to $1,000 a year for a daily drinker.
          </p>

          <p>
            The fair counterpoint is that Ka'Chava is not trying to be a cheap protein. If it replaces a $9 coffee-shop breakfast, it saves money. If it replaces a $1.50 scoop of protein you were already drinking, it costs money. Decide which of those two shakes you are actually replacing before you subscribe.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="vs-protein" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Ka'Chava vs a Plain Plant Protein
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most people searching for a Ka'Chava protein powder are really choosing between Ka'Chava and a straightforward vegan protein like Orgain, Garden of Life Sport or a single-ingredient pea isolate. The difference is what comes along with the protein. A plain plant protein gives you 20 to 30 g of protein in 110 to 170 calories and very little else. Ka'Chava gives you 25 g plus about 120 extra calories of fats, carbohydrates and fiber, and a multivitamin.
          </p>

          <p>
            For lifters and anyone counting calories during a cut, the plain protein wins outright. You want the most protein for the fewest calories and the lowest price, and Ka'Chava loses on all three. Garden of Life Sport, for example, lists about 30 g of protein per serving and carries NSF Certified for Sport status, per the brand at the time of writing, which matters if you compete in a tested sport. Ka'Chava does not carry that certification.
          </p>

          <p>
            For people who struggle to eat breakfast at all, the meal shake wins. A 240-calorie shake with fiber and fat keeps most people fuller than a 120-calorie protein shake, and the vitamin premix covers some gaps in a patchy diet. Buyer feedback on Ka'Chava frequently mentions that it holds them over until lunch, which a scoop of isolate in water rarely does.
          </p>

          <p>
            The middle option is to buy a plain plant protein and build your own meal shake. That gives you control over sugar, calories and fiber and is cheaper, but it takes two minutes and four ingredients instead of one. Our <a href="/best-protein-powder-for-smoothies/">best protein powder for smoothies</a> guide covers the powders that blend best for that approach.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="vs-meal-shakes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Ka'Chava vs Huel and Other Meal Shakes
          </h2>

          <p>
            If you have decided you want a meal shake rather than a protein powder, the obvious rival is Huel. Huel Black Edition is the closest comparison: per Huel's label, a 90 g serving gives about 400 calories and 40 g of plant protein with around 6 g of fiber and 27 vitamins and minerals. That is more protein and more calories per serving, so it is a fuller meal, and it suits people who want a shake to actually replace lunch rather than a light breakfast.
          </p>

          <p>
            Ka'Chava's selling point against Huel is the superfood list and the taste profile. Huel's formulas lean on a simpler, more functional ingredient list, while Ka'Chava emphasizes adaptogens, mushrooms, fruit powders and greens. Whether those extras make a measurable difference at the doses in the blend is doubtful, as covered above, but buyers who like the idea of them will prefer Ka'Chava, and many describe its chocolate as more dessert-like.
          </p>

          <p>
            On protein per serving Huel clearly comes out ahead, with 40 g against 25 g. Compare current prices per serving before you decide on value, because both brands run subscription discounts and Huel's prices vary by product and region. For a more detailed look at Huel's range, including its plain protein products, see our sibling <a href="/huel-protein-powder-review/">Huel protein powder review</a>.
          </p>

          <p>
            Ready-to-drink shakes such as OWYN and Orgain bottles are the other alternative. They are convenient but usually carry less fiber and fewer micronutrients than Ka'Chava, and they are bulky to store. If you mostly want something to drink at your desk, a bottle can make sense. If you want a nutrient-dense breakfast at home, a powder like Ka'Chava or Huel gives you more for the money.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="testing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Testing, Heavy Metals and Safety
          </h2>

          <p>
            Plant proteins attract more attention for heavy metals than whey, because rice, pea and cacao can take up lead and cadmium from soil. That is not a reason to avoid plant protein, but it is a reason to care about testing. Ka'Chava states that it uses an in-house laboratory accredited to ISO 17025 and also sends ingredients to outside labs for testing for heavy metals, pesticides and microbes, per the company's published statements as reported by reviewers.
          </p>

          <p>
            What Ka'Chava does not carry, as far as we could find, is a consumer-facing certification such as NSF Certified for Sport, Informed Choice or Informed Sport. Those programs test finished products against published standards and list them in public databases, which is easier to verify than a brand's own description of its testing. If you compete in a drug-tested sport, choose a certified product instead.
          </p>

          <p>
            Chocolate flavors of any plant protein tend to carry more lead and cadmium than vanilla, because cacao itself is a source. If you drink Ka'Chava daily and heavy metals are a concern, vanilla is the more conservative choice. Our guide to whether <a href="/is-protein-powder-safe/">protein powder is safe</a> covers how to read testing claims and what independent studies have found across the category.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, a note on digestion. Ka'Chava includes digestive enzymes, inulin and around 6 g of fiber, and some buyers report bloating in the first week, which is common when fiber intake rises quickly. Starting with one scoop instead of two for a few days is a sensible approach. Anyone with a medical condition, a history of digestive problems, pregnancy or a long medication list should check with a doctor or registered dietitian before using any meal replacement daily.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Who Should Buy Ka'Chava and Who Should Skip It
          </h2>

          <p>
            Buy Ka'Chava if you skip breakfast or eat it on the run, you want a vegan option, you value convenience over cost, and you like the idea of a single shake that covers protein, fiber and a multivitamin. It is also a sensible choice for travel, when a sachet in a bag beats an airport breakfast, and for people who dislike the thin, sweet taste of many protein shakes.
          </p>

          <p>
            Skip Ka'Chava if protein is your goal. Lifters chasing a daily protein target, people on a tight budget and anyone who already eats a balanced breakfast will get more from a plain protein powder. Skip it as well if you compete in a tested sport, because there is no sport certification, or if you are avoiding added sugar entirely, since there are a few grams of coconut nectar in each serving.
          </p>

          <p>
            Skip it, or at least speak to a professional first, if you plan to replace several meals a day for weight loss. Ka'Chava at 240 calories is light for a meal, and drinking several in place of food can push calories too low. Weight-loss meal replacement plans work best when they are structured, and our <a href="/best-protein-powder-for-weight-loss/">best protein powder for weight loss</a> guide explains how to use protein shakes without cutting food quality.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are on the fence, buy a variety pack or a few single-serve sachets first. The texture is the main reason people stop drinking it, and it is cheaper to find that out with five sachets than with a 15-serving bag and a subscription you forget to cancel.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="where-to-buy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Where to Buy: Amazon, Target, Costco and Direct
          </h2>

          <p>
            Ka'Chava is sold on Amazon in the US, including the full 2 lb, 15-serving bags in chocolate and vanilla, a strawberry bag, single-serve sachet boxes and variety bundles. Buying on Amazon lets you use Subscribe and Save and compare prices quickly, but check that the seller is Ka'Chava or Amazon itself, and that the listing is the 15-serving bag rather than the smaller retail size.
          </p>

          <p>
            The brand's own site offers the widest flavor range and its own subscription discount, which has historically been about $10 off each bag. Target and other grocery chains stock the smaller 15.3 oz bag in core flavors, and Costco has carried a 20-serving chocolate bag in some locations. Retail prices and availability vary by store, so compare cost per serving rather than sticker price.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whichever store you use, the formula is the same, so the decision comes down to price per serving, flavor choice and how easily you can pause or cancel a subscription. If you are not sure you will drink it every day, avoid subscriptions until you have finished your first bag.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you decide Ka'Chava is not for you, our <a href="/best-plant-based-protein-powder/">plant-based protein powder rankings</a> list the vegan powders that give the most protein per dollar, and our <a href="/protein-powder-brands/">protein powder brands</a> overview compares the major names side by side.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying Ka'Chava as a protein supplement</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                At roughly $4 to $4.70 per 25 g of protein, Ka'Chava is one of the most expensive ways to hit a protein target. If you already eat breakfast and just want a post-workout shake, a plain pea or pea-and-rice protein gives the same 25 g for well under half the cost.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating 240 calories as a full meal</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Ka'Chava is light for a meal replacement. Drinking it in place of lunch with nothing else can leave you hungry by mid-afternoon and push total calories too low. Blend it with a banana, oats or nut butter, or pair it with a piece of fruit, if it is replacing a real meal.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming the superfoods are a therapeutic dose</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The adaptogen blend totals about 1 g across six ingredients, and the probiotic count is not disclosed. That is not enough to expect the effects studied in research on individual adaptogens. Treat the extras as a bonus rather than a reason to pay a premium over a simpler shake.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Subscribing before trying the texture</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Ka'Chava is thicker and more earthy than a whey shake, and texture is the most common reason buyers stop. Start with sachets, a small retail bag or a single 15-serving bag, and only set up a subscription once you know you will drink it most days.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Comparing bag prices instead of serving costs</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Ka'Chava sells bags of roughly 7, 15 and 20 servings depending on the store, and retailers price them differently. Divide the price by the servings on the label before comparing, or you may pay more per shake for what looks like a cheaper bag.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="faq-list">
            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is Ka'Chava a protein powder?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Not exactly. Ka'Chava is a meal shake that contains protein. Each two-scoop serving gives about 25 g of plant protein in roughly 240 calories, per the label at the time of writing, alongside fiber, fats, carbohydrates and a vitamin and mineral premix. A dedicated protein powder delivers similar protein in about half the calories and at a much lower cost per gram.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>How much protein is in Ka'Chava?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  About 25 g per two-scoop serving, per the label at the time of writing, from a blend of yellow pea protein, organic brown rice protein, amaranth, quinoa and sacha inchi. The blend is proprietary, so the brand does not publish how much comes from each source or a full amino acid profile, which means leucine per serving cannot be confirmed from the label.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is Ka'Chava good for weight loss?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  It can help if it replaces a higher-calorie breakfast, because 240 calories with 25 g of protein and about 6 g of fiber is reasonably filling. It is not a weight-loss product on its own, and replacing several meals a day can push calories too low. For structured weight loss, a doctor or registered dietitian can help you plan meals safely.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Does Ka'Chava contain sugar?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Yes, a little. Expect roughly 8 g of total sugar per serving, with about 4 to 6 g added from organic coconut flower nectar depending on flavor, per the label at the time of writing. It is also sweetened with monk fruit extract and contains no sucralose, aspartame or stevia, per the brand and retailer listings.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is Ka'Chava third-party tested?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Ka'Chava says it tests in an ISO 17025-accredited in-house lab and sends ingredients to outside labs for heavy metals, pesticides and microbes. We could not find NSF Certified for Sport, Informed Choice or Informed Sport listings for it. Athletes in drug-tested sports should choose a product listed in one of those public databases instead.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Can you buy Ka'Chava on Amazon?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Yes. Ka'Chava is sold on Amazon in the US, including 2 lb, 15-serving bags in chocolate and vanilla, a strawberry bag, single-serve sachets and variety bundles. Check that the seller is Ka'Chava or Amazon, compare the serving count on the listing, and use Subscribe and Save only after you know you like the taste and texture.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>What is a cheaper alternative to Ka'Chava?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  If you want protein, a plain plant protein such as Orgain or a single-ingredient pea isolate costs far less per 25 g. If you want a meal shake, Huel Black Edition gives about 40 g of protein and 400 calories per serving, so compare its current price per serving against Ka'Chava's before you buy. A homemade smoothie with protein powder, chia and fruit is cheapest of all.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

          </div>
        </div>

